e American Sociological Association (ASA), established in 1905, is a pivotal institution in the field of sociology, headquartered in Washington, D.C. This nonprofit organization emerged from a growing need to professionalize and unify sociological research and practice across the United States at a time when the discipline was gaining traction amid significant social transformations. Key figures in its founding, such as Edward A. Ross and Albion W. Small, were instrumental in shaping the association's objectives, which aimed to promote the study of society and foster the application of sociological insights to social issues.

